WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.038
Module 9: briefing executives.

00:00:02.038 --> 00:00:06.996
By the end, you'll be able to turn a
sixty-slide report into a ten-minute

00:00:06.996 --> 00:00:09.576
conversation that leads to a decision,

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:12.701
say honestly what you
know and what you don't,

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:17.048
handle the reactions you'll hear
in almost every executive room,

00:00:17.048 --> 00:00:21.734
and open a 360 debrief so the leader
hears development, not judgment.

00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.148
Executives don't want
a tour of the report.

00:00:25.148 --> 00:00:26.707
They want three things.

00:00:26.707 --> 00:00:29.826
What's going on, said
plainly in one sentence.

00:00:29.826 --> 00:00:33.893
How sure are you, so they know
how much weight to put on it.

00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:38.164
And what do you need from us: a
decision, an owner, a resource.

00:00:38.164 --> 00:00:40.604
The report is fifty to sixty slides.

00:00:40.604 --> 00:00:44.604
Your briefing is three to five
exhibits and a conversation.

00:00:44.604 --> 00:00:45.892
Here's a good test.

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:48.671
If the executives left after ten minutes,

00:00:48.671 --> 00:00:53.552
could each of them repeat your headline
and the decision they agreed to?

00:00:53.552 --> 00:00:55.383
If not, it was a data tour.

00:00:55.867 --> 00:01:01.240
the ADDIS methodology runs Assess,
Dialogue, Design, Implement, Sustain.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.777
A Mosaic report closes out Assess.

00:01:03.777 --> 00:01:05.941
Your briefing opens Dialogue.

00:01:05.941 --> 00:01:07.583
That changes your job.

00:01:07.583 --> 00:01:11.165
You're not there to deliver
a verdict and leave.

00:01:11.165 --> 00:01:15.419
You're there to give leaders
an accurate, shared picture,

00:01:15.419 --> 00:01:20.718
help them make sense of it together,
including the uncomfortable parts,

00:01:20.718 --> 00:01:24.300
and agree what matters
most, which feeds Design.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.613
So leave room for conversation.

00:01:26.613 --> 00:01:32.360
A ten-minute briefing inside a
sixty-to-ninety-minute session is about right.

00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:36.689
Here's the shape of almost
every good Mosaic briefing.

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:42.493
Start with thirty seconds of context: what
you measured, how many people answered,

00:01:42.493 --> 00:01:45.253
and how much weight the data can carry.

00:01:45.253 --> 00:01:50.137
Then one headline sentence, the
thing you most want them to remember.

00:01:50.137 --> 00:01:53.251
Then three findings,
about two minutes each.

00:01:53.251 --> 00:01:57.215
Each one gets a plain claim,
one exhibit, and a so-what.

00:01:57.215 --> 00:02:00.329
Spend one minute on what
you don't know yet,

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.434
because naming the limits is
what makes the rest credible.

00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:09.530
Then one clear ask: a decision, an
owner, and when you'll measure again.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:10.238
That's it.

00:02:10.238 --> 00:02:13.069
Everything else in the report is backup.

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:17.113
The headline is the hardest
sentence you'll write.

00:02:17.113 --> 00:02:21.937
A good one is a single sentence of
about twenty-five words or fewer.

00:02:21.937 --> 00:02:27.754
It's about the organization, not the survey:
"our people", not "the survey shows".

00:02:27.754 --> 00:02:31.442
It holds a tension or an
insight, not just a number.

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:36.337
And it's fair to the data, so every
word can be backed by an exhibit.

00:02:36.337 --> 00:02:38.749
A list of scores isn't a headline.

00:02:38.749 --> 00:02:43.501
Neither is "Northwind's culture is
in trouble", or a response rate.

00:02:43.501 --> 00:02:47.899
Here's a strong one: Northwind's
people are united by purpose,

00:02:47.899 --> 00:02:53.291
but since the merger they feel disconnected
across sites and shut out of the

00:02:53.291 --> 00:02:55.206
decisions that affect them.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:59.834
You're briefing Northwind's executives
on the October progress check.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:01.212
Pick the best headline.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:07.638
Now watch a Mosaic practitioner brief
Northwind's executive team on the February

00:03:07.638 --> 00:03:08.751
Culture Mosaic.

00:03:08.751 --> 00:03:10.681
It runs about ten minutes.

00:03:10.681 --> 00:03:15.580
As you watch, notice the structure,
the language about confidence,

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.549
and how every finding ends in a so-what.

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:20.994
Thank you for the time.

00:03:20.994 --> 00:03:27.897
In February and early March we invited
all 640 of your people to take the Culture

00:03:27.897 --> 00:03:28.494
Mosaic.

00:03:28.494 --> 00:03:34.375
412 responded, which is 64%, so this
is a broad and reliable picture.

00:03:34.375 --> 00:03:37.187
The scores can carry real weight.

00:03:37.187 --> 00:03:40.255
Where a group is small, I'll say so.

00:03:40.255 --> 00:03:42.897
And where you see a percentile,

00:03:42.897 --> 00:03:49.631
that's Northwind compared with the other
organizations in the Mosaic benchmark.

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.451
If you remember one thing from today,

00:03:52.451 --> 00:03:56.044
make it this: Northwind's
people are united by purpose,

00:03:56.044 --> 00:04:01.010
but since the merger they feel disconnected
across sites and shut out of the

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:02.774
decisions that affect them.

00:04:02.774 --> 00:04:07.347
I'll show you three findings behind
that, then what we don't know yet,

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:10.484
and then the decision I'd
like us to leave with.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.936
First, the strength, and it's a big one.

00:04:13.936 --> 00:04:16.593
Purpose is your highest tile at 82.

00:04:16.593 --> 00:04:22.742
The statement 'Our purpose is well understood
and clearly describes why we exist'

00:04:22.742 --> 00:04:28.587
sits at the 88th percentile, so very
few organizations in the benchmark score

00:04:28.587 --> 00:04:29.119
higher.

00:04:29.119 --> 00:04:35.343
And in the comments, the most common thing
people want to keep is their commitment

00:04:35.343 --> 00:04:36.254
to patients.

00:04:36.654 --> 00:04:39.059
But look right next to it.

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:40.448
Strategy is 51.

00:04:40.448 --> 00:04:43.501
People know why Northwind exists.

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:47.758
They're much less sure
how it's getting there.

00:04:47.758 --> 00:04:53.772
And this is where it gets interesting:
leaders score Strategy 72,

00:04:53.772 --> 00:04:56.641
and everyone else scores it 47.

00:04:56.641 --> 00:04:58.677
That's a 25-point gap.

00:04:58.677 --> 00:05:03.211
In this room, One Northwind
probably feels clear.

00:05:03.211 --> 00:05:05.894
On the floor, it doesn't yet.

00:05:06.294 --> 00:05:06.816
So what?

00:05:06.816 --> 00:05:10.602
Your purpose is an asset you
can build the integration on.

00:05:10.602 --> 00:05:14.650
But the strategy hasn't been
translated for the frontline yet,

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.893
and that's a gap leaders are
unlikely to see from where they sit.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:25.466
Second: collaboration across the
merged sites is your weakest point.

00:05:25.466 --> 00:05:29.094
Collaboration is your lowest tile at 44.

00:05:29.094 --> 00:05:31.996
It isn't low everywhere, though.

00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:34.897
Admin and Corporate score it 61,

00:05:34.897 --> 00:05:39.703
while Nursing scores it 38
and the Clinic Network 36.

00:05:39.703 --> 00:05:44.963
Those are big groups, 161 and
97 people, so this is solid.

00:05:44.963 --> 00:05:50.132
The Clinic Network is also
lowest on Change Ready, at 39.

00:05:50.531 --> 00:05:52.404
The comments tell us why.

00:05:52.404 --> 00:05:58.398
The most frequent improvement theme is
cross-site coordination since the merger.

00:05:58.398 --> 00:06:04.241
And one specific behaviour stands out:
the statement about disagreements being

00:06:04.241 --> 00:06:08.287
openly explored and managed
is at the 14th percentile.

00:06:08.287 --> 00:06:12.781
Teams aren't working through
disagreement across site lines.

00:06:13.181 --> 00:06:13.684
So what?

00:06:13.684 --> 00:06:17.834
The integration's hardest work is
between nursing and the clinics,

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.531
at the seams of the merger.

00:06:19.531 --> 00:06:21.606
That's where to put effort first.

00:06:22.006 --> 00:06:29.589
Third, and this one is harder to hear: the
frontline feels less voice than leaders

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:30.976
believe it has.

00:06:30.976 --> 00:06:33.011
Empowered Teams is 49.

00:06:33.011 --> 00:06:38.929
Leaders score it 70, and everyone
else 45, another 25-point gap.

00:06:38.929 --> 00:06:45.310
The statement 'Speaking up is done
regularly by people at all levels,

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:50.119
even on difficult topics'
is at the 21st percentile.

00:06:50.119 --> 00:06:57.794
And 'decisions made without frontline input'
came up several times in the comments.

00:06:58.193 --> 00:07:02.058
Your Psychological Safety
Index is 3 on a 5-point scale.

00:07:02.058 --> 00:07:07.648
The report describes that as acceptable
to attempt change, but a risk to success.

00:07:07.648 --> 00:07:12.410
I'd add that you're at the cautious
end of that band, not the middle.

00:07:12.809 --> 00:07:13.342
So what?

00:07:13.342 --> 00:07:17.007
Change efforts depend on
people raising problems early.

00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:20.073
Right now, many don't
feel safe or invited to.

00:07:20.073 --> 00:07:24.338
That's a risk for every integration
decision you make this year.

00:07:24.739 --> 00:07:26.222
Three honest limits.

00:07:26.222 --> 00:07:32.153
First, this is one snapshot, so we can't
yet say whether things are improving or

00:07:32.153 --> 00:07:33.191
getting worse.

00:07:33.191 --> 00:07:38.455
Second, the survey tells us what
people experience but only partly why.

00:07:38.455 --> 00:07:41.198
The comments help, but they're short.

00:07:41.198 --> 00:07:47.352
Third, a few small groups were combined
into 'Other' to protect people's anonymity,

00:07:47.352 --> 00:07:51.207
so we deliberately can't
speak to them on their own.

00:07:51.207 --> 00:07:55.582
And some of the biggest themes,
like staffing and workload,

00:07:55.582 --> 00:07:58.102
sit outside the culture framework.

00:07:58.102 --> 00:08:01.290
They're real and they're
on people's minds,

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.701
but this survey doesn't
measure them in depth.

00:08:05.106 --> 00:08:07.778
So here's what I'd like
us to decide today.

00:08:07.778 --> 00:08:12.314
The report suggests starting with your
three lowest tiles: Collaboration,

00:08:12.314 --> 00:08:14.365
Change Ready and Empowered Teams.

00:08:14.365 --> 00:08:19.212
I'd recommend we commit to those three,
with an owner for each from this team,

00:08:19.212 --> 00:08:23.935
and agree to re-measure just those three
in about six to eight months with a

00:08:23.935 --> 00:08:28.160
progress check, so we can show the
board whether anything has moved.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.132
And one more thing: let's agree how and
when we'll share these results back with

00:08:33.132 --> 00:08:33.877
your people.

00:08:33.877 --> 00:08:38.103
They gave us their time, and they'll
be watching what we do with it.

00:08:38.506 --> 00:08:42.497
Before we talk about what to
do, what in this surprises you?

00:08:42.497 --> 00:08:43.827
And what feels true?

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:46.448
Here's why that example works.

00:08:46.448 --> 00:08:52.211
The context earns trust: the number who
answered and the response rate come first,

00:08:52.211 --> 00:08:54.741
with a promise to flag small groups.

00:08:54.741 --> 00:08:59.660
Every number names its measure:
Purpose is a tile score of eighty-two,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:03.385
and a statement sits at the
eighty-eighth percentile.

00:09:03.385 --> 00:09:06.196
Never "Purpose is eighty-eight percent".

00:09:06.196 --> 00:09:08.305
It leads with a real strength,

00:09:08.305 --> 00:09:12.100
but not as a cushion: the
strength becomes an insight.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:16.597
Each finding ends in a so-what,
which is the bridge into Design.

00:09:16.597 --> 00:09:21.939
The hard message is said carefully: it
describes a pattern and names no one.

00:09:21.939 --> 00:09:26.226
The limits come before the ask,
which makes the ask credible.

00:09:26.226 --> 00:09:31.989
And the ask is specific: three priorities,
an owner each, a date to measure again,

00:09:31.989 --> 00:09:34.730
and a commitment to share results back.

00:09:35.239 --> 00:09:39.783
Use one exhibit per finding, plus
at most one or two for context.

00:09:39.783 --> 00:09:44.886
For each finding, ask which single
view makes it obvious in five seconds.

00:09:44.886 --> 00:09:48.032
The headline diamond
shows the overall shape.

00:09:48.032 --> 00:09:51.737
A dimension's three tiles
side by side show a spread.

00:09:51.737 --> 00:09:54.603
The leader comparison shows a leader gap.

00:09:54.603 --> 00:09:57.470
A cropped heatmap shows where it's worse.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.266
One item row shows a specific behaviour.

00:10:00.266 --> 00:10:03.621
And the progress summary
shows change over time.

00:10:03.621 --> 00:10:06.138
Keep the full report as an appendix,

00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:09.773
and know it well enough to
find any page in seconds.

00:10:10.273 --> 00:10:12.891
Report pages are built to be complete.

00:10:12.891 --> 00:10:15.302
A briefing slide needs to be clear.

00:10:15.302 --> 00:10:20.883
Slide surgery means cutting a report page
down to the one point it needs to make,

00:10:20.883 --> 00:10:22.813
without changing any number.

00:10:22.813 --> 00:10:28.256
Here, seventy-two shaded cells become
two rows, Collaboration and Change Ready,

00:10:28.256 --> 00:10:33.010
across the named departments, with
group sizes and a one-line source.

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:37.075
The rules: the title is the
finding, written as a sentence.

00:10:37.075 --> 00:10:39.073
Remove what doesn't serve it.

00:10:39.073 --> 00:10:44.448
Never change, re-round or recolour a
number in a way that changes its meaning.

00:10:44.448 --> 00:10:46.859
Keep N and the source on the slide.

00:10:46.859 --> 00:10:50.029
Never add back anything
the report suppressed.

00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:54.576
And if anyone asks what you cut,
the full page is in the appendix.

00:10:55.073 --> 00:10:58.782
Match each Northwind finding
to the single best exhibit.

00:10:59.273 --> 00:11:02.476
Small wording choices
carry a lot of weight.

00:11:02.476 --> 00:11:05.679
Don't say "Purpose is
eighty-eight percent".

00:11:05.679 --> 00:11:09.755
Say "that statement is at the
eighty-eighth percentile".

00:11:09.755 --> 00:11:12.813
Don't say "sixty percent of people agree".

00:11:12.813 --> 00:11:16.671
Say "it scores sixty on a
zero-to-one-hundred scale".

00:11:16.671 --> 00:11:20.747
Swap "the data proves" for
"the data strongly suggests",

00:11:20.747 --> 00:11:23.514
or "three sources point the same way".

00:11:23.514 --> 00:11:28.100
Don't say "the huddles improved
collaboration by eight points".

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:32.322
Say "collaboration improved
meaningfully, by eight points,

00:11:32.322 --> 00:11:34.724
over the period the huddles ran".

00:11:34.724 --> 00:11:36.690
Don't say "it didn't work".

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.019
Say "we can't see a change yet".

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:43.169
And instead of "you're blind
to how you treat your team",

00:11:43.169 --> 00:11:48.847
try "here's a place where your team's
experience differs from how you see it".

00:11:48.847 --> 00:11:50.812
The full bank is on screen.

00:11:51.306 --> 00:11:53.649
Name the pattern, not the person.

00:11:53.649 --> 00:11:57.269
Culture data describes how
things work around here.

00:11:57.269 --> 00:11:59.754
It doesn't tell you who's to blame.

00:11:59.754 --> 00:12:03.800
In a group briefing, talk
about tiles, groups and themes,

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:06.284
never named or identifiable people.

00:12:06.284 --> 00:12:09.834
Never connect a group result
to a manager by name.

00:12:09.834 --> 00:12:14.377
And keep individual 360 results
out of group briefings entirely,

00:12:14.377 --> 00:12:17.429
unless a leader chooses
to share their own.

00:12:17.429 --> 00:12:19.772
Candor and care aren't opposites.

00:12:19.772 --> 00:12:22.966
Say the hard thing
clearly, about the system.

00:12:23.473 --> 00:12:26.024
Pushback is a sign the data matters.

00:12:26.024 --> 00:12:30.418
Treat it as the start of Dialogue,
not an attack on your work.

00:12:30.418 --> 00:12:31.977
Respond in four steps.

00:12:31.977 --> 00:12:34.812
Acknowledge: take the concern seriously.

00:12:34.812 --> 00:12:39.064
Clarify: say accurately what
the data does and doesn't show.

00:12:39.064 --> 00:12:42.466
Hold the line, politely
and without apologising,

00:12:42.466 --> 00:12:46.009
on anything that protects
respondents or accuracy.

00:12:46.009 --> 00:12:50.474
Then redirect to the decision:
what would help you act on this?

00:12:50.474 --> 00:12:54.585
The next six screens cover the
reactions you'll hear most.

00:12:55.073 --> 00:12:58.408
Priya Natarajan, the CFO,
says: I don't buy it.

00:12:58.408 --> 00:13:02.452
The people who answer these
surveys are the unhappy ones.

00:13:02.452 --> 00:13:04.581
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:05.073 --> 00:13:07.537
Sam Whitfield, the Chief Nursing Officer,

00:13:07.537 --> 00:13:11.864
asks who wrote the comment about
decisions made without frontline input.

00:13:11.864 --> 00:13:13.667
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:14.173 --> 00:13:19.495
Dana Okafor, the CEO, says: just
tell me which team is the problem.

00:13:19.495 --> 00:13:21.879
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:22.433 --> 00:13:26.836
Alex Moreau asks how Northwind
compares with other health systems,

00:13:26.836 --> 00:13:28.504
and what a good score is.

00:13:28.504 --> 00:13:30.505
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:31.005 --> 00:13:36.860
Jordan Reyes, the COO, wants to fix
collaboration by next quarter and re-survey

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:39.231
everyone in January to prove it.

00:13:39.231 --> 00:13:41.454
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:41.939 --> 00:13:47.245
In a private debrief, Jordan looks at the
Empowered Teams results and says a couple

00:13:47.245 --> 00:13:50.313
of people with an axe to
grind have skewed them.

00:13:50.313 --> 00:13:52.231
Choose the strongest response.

00:13:52.739 --> 00:13:55.842
A 360 debrief is a different conversation.

00:13:55.842 --> 00:14:00.127
It's private, it's personal,
and the leader owns the data.

00:14:00.127 --> 00:14:06.111
Before you start, remind them what it's
for: development, not performance review,

00:14:06.111 --> 00:14:07.367
pay or promotion.

00:14:07.367 --> 00:14:09.436
Explain who's in the report.

00:14:09.436 --> 00:14:09.805
Self.

00:14:09.805 --> 00:14:13.278
Their boss, or up to two
bosses, who are named,

00:14:13.278 --> 00:14:16.528
because the leader knows
who they report to.

00:14:16.528 --> 00:14:21.257
And peers, direct reports and
others as groups of three or more.

00:14:21.257 --> 00:14:25.246
Smaller groups are folded
and their comments withheld,

00:14:25.246 --> 00:14:28.128
so never speculate about who said what.

00:14:28.128 --> 00:14:30.713
Then let them read before you talk.

00:14:30.713 --> 00:14:32.413
Ask more than you tell.

00:14:32.413 --> 00:14:38.249
Start with hidden strengths, because
they're easy to hear and genuinely useful.

00:14:38.249 --> 00:14:41.500
Pick one or two development
themes, not ten.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:46.081
And end with the leader's own
commitments, in their own words.

00:14:46.572 --> 00:14:50.117
Now watch the first ten minutes
of Jordan Reyes's debrief.

00:14:50.117 --> 00:14:51.523
Watch for a few things.

00:14:51.523 --> 00:14:54.212
The coach doesn't open
with the biggest gap,

00:14:54.212 --> 00:14:57.269
never uses "blind spot" as
a label for the person,

00:14:57.269 --> 00:15:01.975
doesn't argue with Jordan's first
reaction, and doesn't guess who rated what.

00:15:01.975 --> 00:15:07.048
And notice how the strength and the
development area turn out to be the same trait,

00:15:07.048 --> 00:15:08.332
seen from two angles.

00:15:08.839 --> 00:15:11.379
Thanks for making the time, Jordan.

00:15:11.379 --> 00:15:14.573
Before we look at
anything, a few reminders.

00:15:14.573 --> 00:15:16.098
This report is yours.

00:15:16.098 --> 00:15:20.526
It's for your development, not
for performance review or pay.

00:15:20.526 --> 00:15:25.897
Your raters were Dana as your boss,
and you'll see Dana's ratings by name,

00:15:25.897 --> 00:15:28.438
because you know who you report to.

00:15:28.438 --> 00:15:33.011
Then four peers and seven direct
reports, shown only as groups.

00:15:33.011 --> 00:15:36.786
You also invited two people
in the 'Other' category.

00:15:36.786 --> 00:15:42.666
With fewer than three, their answers aren't
in the report and their comments were

00:15:42.666 --> 00:15:43.319
withheld.

00:15:43.319 --> 00:15:48.981
That protects everyone's anonymity, so
we won't try to guess what anyone said.

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:49.634
Sound OK?

00:15:50.039 --> 00:15:54.594
Let's start with something others
see more clearly than you do.

00:15:54.594 --> 00:15:57.269
On Decisions, you rated yourself 5.0.

00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:02.909
Everyone else rated you 6.0, and your
direct reports gave 6.3 on using data in

00:16:02.909 --> 00:16:03.632
decisions.

00:16:03.632 --> 00:16:08.115
That's around the 82nd percentile
compared with other leaders.

00:16:08.115 --> 00:16:14.044
Your team describes you as 'a sharp,
data-driven operator who makes timely calls'.

00:16:14.044 --> 00:16:17.949
Why do you think you rate
yourself lower than they do?

00:16:20.345 --> 00:16:22.296
Now let's look at Adapt and Enable.

00:16:22.296 --> 00:16:26.031
Take a minute with the comparison
page and tell me what you notice.

00:16:28.427 --> 00:16:30.103
You've spotted it.

00:16:30.103 --> 00:16:35.132
On Change Ready, you rate
yourself 6.2 and others 4.4.

00:16:35.132 --> 00:16:38.390
On Empowered Teams, 5.6 versus 4.1.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:45.281
Your direct reports rate 'creating
space for others to contribute' at 3.4,

00:16:45.281 --> 00:16:48.633
which is around the 12th percentile.

00:16:48.633 --> 00:16:51.147
What's your first reaction?

00:16:53.543 --> 00:16:55.863
Here's something I find interesting.

00:16:55.863 --> 00:16:59.537
The thing people value most,
fast, data-driven decisions,

00:16:59.537 --> 00:17:02.759
may be connected to the
thing they'd like more of.

00:17:02.759 --> 00:17:07.013
Your direct reports said they'd
like more say before changes land,

00:17:07.013 --> 00:17:11.975
and that integration decisions can feel
finished before they hear about them.

00:17:11.975 --> 00:17:16.422
One comment suggested that urgency
can close down debate in meetings.

00:17:16.422 --> 00:17:19.966
Does that ring true in any
situations you can think of?

00:17:20.366 --> 00:17:25.700
And notice: Dana rates you 5.5 on
Empowered Teams, and your team 3.6.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.334
They're seeing different
parts of how you work.

00:17:29.334 --> 00:17:33.199
What might your team
experience that Dana doesn't?

00:17:33.611 --> 00:17:37.723
If you picked one thing to experiment
with over the next three months,

00:17:37.723 --> 00:17:38.722
what would it be?

00:17:38.722 --> 00:17:41.071
… And how would you know it was working?

00:17:41.578 --> 00:17:42.698
Pause and reflect.

00:17:42.698 --> 00:17:46.555
Think about the last time you
gave someone difficult feedback.

00:17:46.555 --> 00:17:48.608
Did you talk more or listen more?

00:17:48.608 --> 00:17:51.843
Did you start with what was
hard or what was strong?

00:17:51.843 --> 00:17:55.638
And what's one habit from the
talk-track you'd like to adopt?

00:17:55.638 --> 00:17:57.069
Your notes are private.

00:17:57.578 --> 00:18:02.620
People who took the survey will judge
it by one thing: did anything happen?

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:05.510
Sharing results back,
quickly and honestly,

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:09.006
protects next year's response
rate and builds trust.

00:18:09.006 --> 00:18:13.846
A good share-back goes out within a
few weeks of the executive briefing.

00:18:13.846 --> 00:18:16.938
It shares the headline
and the three findings,

00:18:16.938 --> 00:18:19.694
including at least one uncomfortable one.

00:18:19.694 --> 00:18:24.400
It says what leaders have committed
to, and when you'll measure again.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:27.022
And it uses only what the report shows.

00:18:27.022 --> 00:18:30.450
Recommend that the client's
own leaders deliver it.

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:31.929
It's their commitment.

00:18:32.505 --> 00:18:37.025
SurveyCraft's Action plan turns
priorities into a tracked list.

00:18:37.025 --> 00:18:42.048
It sits on the survey's Responses
page and starts by listing the three

00:18:42.048 --> 00:18:45.707
lowest-scoring areas; for
Northwind, Collaboration,

00:18:45.707 --> 00:18:48.074
Change Ready and Empowered Teams.

00:18:48.074 --> 00:18:50.442
Pick two or three as focus areas.

00:18:50.442 --> 00:18:55.034
For each, write what you'll do,
and add an owner and a due date,

00:18:55.034 --> 00:18:57.329
then tick it off when it's done.

00:18:57.329 --> 00:18:59.195
Then agree the re-measure.

00:18:59.195 --> 00:19:03.213
The plan links straight to
setting up a follow-up pulse,

00:19:03.213 --> 00:19:08.091
and a Progress Monitor on the same
domains is the natural follow-up.

00:19:08.091 --> 00:19:11.463
The lowest scores are a
suggestion, not a rule.

00:19:11.463 --> 00:19:13.974
Ask which priority, if it improved,

00:19:13.974 --> 00:19:18.925
would make the biggest difference
to what the client is trying to do.

00:19:19.439 --> 00:19:21.776
Don't end with "any questions?".

00:19:21.776 --> 00:19:23.164
End with four asks.

00:19:23.164 --> 00:19:27.765
A decision: which two or three
priorities you're committing to.

00:19:27.765 --> 00:19:30.467
An owner: a named executive for each.

00:19:30.467 --> 00:19:33.170
The next measurement: what, and when,

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:36.602
usually a progress check
in six to nine months.

00:19:36.602 --> 00:19:42.153
And the share-back: how and when results
go back to the people who answered.

00:19:42.153 --> 00:19:47.703
Write them on the last slide, and confirm
them out loud before people leave.

00:19:48.272 --> 00:19:49.464
Now it's your turn.

00:19:49.464 --> 00:19:51.973
Build and record a five-minute briefing.

00:19:51.973 --> 00:19:54.482
It isn't graded and nothing is uploaded,

00:19:54.482 --> 00:19:57.995
so treat it as your rehearsal
for the capstone briefing.

00:19:57.995 --> 00:20:02.198
Choose either a progress-check
briefing for Northwind's executives,

00:20:02.198 --> 00:20:04.770
or the opening of Jordan Reyes's debrief.

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:09.662
Fill in the template, prepare up to three
exhibits with slide surgery applied,

00:20:09.662 --> 00:20:14.430
record five to six minutes, and score
yourself against the rubric on screen.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.876
It's the same rubric the capstone uses.

00:20:17.372 --> 00:20:21.987
Time for the module check: eight
questions, and you need seven right.

00:20:21.987 --> 00:20:23.459
Retakes are unlimited.

00:20:23.972 --> 00:20:26.598
Here's the one-three-one
briefing template.

00:20:26.598 --> 00:20:29.223
Fill it in before you
build a single slide.

00:20:29.739 --> 00:20:33.942
And the pushback playbook: what
you'll hear, the line to hold,

00:20:33.942 --> 00:20:35.434
and a sample response.

00:20:35.939 --> 00:20:38.132
You're ready for the capstone.

00:20:38.132 --> 00:20:39.814
Brief in one-three-one.

00:20:39.814 --> 00:20:45.077
Pick one exhibit per finding, and cut
it down without changing a number.

00:20:45.077 --> 00:20:49.829
Say what you know, how sure you
are, and what you don't know yet.

00:20:49.829 --> 00:20:54.581
Meet pushback with acknowledge,
clarify, hold the line, redirect.

00:20:54.581 --> 00:20:59.991
In a 360 debrief, ask more than you
tell, and start with hidden strengths.

00:20:59.991 --> 00:21:05.694
And close with a decision, an owner, the
next measurement, and the share-back.

00:21:05.694 --> 00:21:07.887
Next: Module 10, the capstone.

